\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces The two incorrect outcomes available to a system that keeps no durable record of workflow progress. Restarting the process duplicates side effects that have already happened; abandoning it loses the work that remains. Neither is acceptable when the completed steps have moved money.\relax }}{2}{figure.caption.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces Infrastructure components required by published workflow orchestrators, compared with the engine designed in this report. Counts are taken from each system's own deployment documentation and are listed again, with sources, in Table\nobreakspace {}\ref {tab:orchestrators}.\relax }}{5}{figure.caption.12}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Positioning of the surveyed systems on the two axes this project cares about. Systems in the upper half require a coordination service outside their data store; systems in the right half partition execution across machines. The lower-right quadrant --- partitioned without a coordination service --- is the target of this work.\relax }}{16}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces The crash-induced, semantically duplicate side effect that exact-match idempotency cannot detect. Completed steps resolve from history and are not re-run, but a planning step that re-executes at a non-zero sampling temperature re-issues the same intent in different words, producing a hash that matches no recorded key.\relax }}{20}{figure.caption.20}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces The container topology against which the requirements of this chapter are sized, and on which every measurement reported by this project was taken. Three engine nodes share one PostgreSQL leader with a streaming standby; the embedder and the external-effect simulator are test-only services and are never exposed beyond the host.\relax }}{30}{figure.caption.27}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces The five architectural tiers of the engine. Every node runs the client-facing gateway, the execution tier and the control tier in one process; only the persistence tier is shared. The control tier is where every mutating path is gated by a fencing-token check, which is what makes partitioned execution safe without a coordination service.\relax }}{36}{figure.caption.30}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces The two-level mapping. The first mapping is fixed for the lifetime of a workflow; only the second changes when the cluster is resized. This is what reduces rebalancing from a data migration to an ownership handoff.\relax }}{39}{figure.caption.33}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Why the lease alone is insufficient and the in-transaction token check is what provides safety. Without fencing, a node that was frozen past its lease expiry commits alongside the new owner and produces split-brain. With fencing, it commits nothing.\relax }}{42}{figure.caption.35}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces The six tables holding every piece of durable state in the system. Every table carries \texttt {shard\_id}, which is what sharding physically is in this design; no statement is ever issued without it.\relax }}{46}{figure.caption.36}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces The workflow task loop. Step 8 is a single transaction, which is what makes the system safe; step 9 discards the in-memory state, which is why correctness never depends on retaining it.\relax }}{50}{figure.caption.38}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces A worked trace across three tasks. The crucial moment is the writing of sequence 5: the \emph {intent} to charge is durably recorded before any money moves, which is the entire basis of the engine's safety.\relax }}{53}{figure.caption.40}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.7}{\ignorespaces Every place a crash can land during one activity. Three of the four windows recover safely without intervention. Window C is irreducible, and the design says so rather than claiming exactly-once execution.\relax }}{58}{figure.caption.42}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.8}{\ignorespaces Saga compensation. The stack exists only in memory and is rebuilt by replay; compensations run in reverse order as ordinary activities; and a compensation that exhausts its retries leaves the workflow in a terminal state requiring human intervention.\relax }}{61}{figure.caption.45}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.9}{\ignorespaces The semantic idempotency decision path. The non-deterministic verdict is computed once in new mode and journalled; every subsequent replay reads the recorded verdict and performs no embedding call, no similarity computation and no network access.\relax }}{64}{figure.caption.46}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.10}{\ignorespaces The goroutine composition of one node. Worker counts are per owned shard, so a node's concurrency scales with the number of shards it owns rather than with cluster size.\relax }}{67}{figure.caption.48}%
